1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,114
Shine white light on a surface with fine,

2
00:00:03,154 --> 00:00:07,349
regular grooves, like a CD, and it fans out into rainbows.

3
00:00:07,419 --> 00:00:11,098
Stack thin, clear layers, like those in a morpho butterfly's wing,

4
00:00:11,138 --> 00:00:13,537
and they reflect one vivid colour.

5
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:15,914
No pigment anywhere. So how can mere

6
00:00:15,954 --> 00:00:18,176
structure pick a direction, and a colour?

7
00:00:18,246 --> 00:00:20,974
It all comes down to one idea: symmetry.

8
00:00:21,794 --> 00:00:24,003
Start with the simplest structure:

9
00:00:24,043 --> 00:00:26,996
a flat boundary, with glass below and air above.

10
00:00:27,066 --> 00:00:32,071
Remember from episode one: a plane wave's tilt is set by kx,

11
00:00:32,111 --> 00:00:36,633
its frequency along x. Then sin θ = kx/k.

12
00:00:36,703 --> 00:00:40,188
In glass the wavelength shrinks, so k grows to n·k0,

13
00:00:40,228 --> 00:00:45,632
where n is the refractive index. Each medium gets its own circle.

14
00:00:45,702 --> 00:00:47,287
The field can't jump at the boundary,

15
00:00:47,327 --> 00:00:49,721
so the crests on both sides must meet.

16
00:00:49,761 --> 00:00:54,345
Both waves share one kx: it's conserved, like momentum.

17
00:00:54,415 --> 00:00:57,651
In k-space, a shared kx is one vertical line.

18
00:00:57,691 --> 00:00:59,286
Where it crosses each circle,

19
00:00:59,326 --> 00:01:04,139
you read off each wave's direction. That's Snell's law, in one picture.

20
00:01:04,209 --> 00:01:07,939
Now tilt the incoming wave further. The line slides outward,

21
00:01:07,979 --> 00:01:10,751
and soon it misses the air circle completely.

22
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:15,194
No wave in air has that kx, so all the light reflects,

23
00:01:15,234 --> 00:01:19,794
leaving only an evanescent tail. That's total internal reflection.

24
00:01:20,614 --> 00:01:24,461
Now give the boundary a pattern that repeats every Λ,

25
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:27,205
the period. That's a grating.

26
00:01:27,275 --> 00:01:31,519
Anything that repeats like this is a Fourier series:

27
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:36,972
a sum of waves e^(imKx), where K = 2π/Λ.

28
00:01:37,042 --> 00:01:40,650
Passing through the grating multiplies the light by these waves,

29
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:43,940
and each one shifts kx by mK.

30
00:01:44,010 --> 00:01:48,435
So kx is now conserved only up to multiples of K.

31
00:01:48,475 --> 00:01:52,614
In k-space: copies of the line, spaced by K.

32
00:01:52,684 --> 00:01:54,805
Each copy that crosses the circle is a

33
00:01:54,845 --> 00:01:57,393
beam that can leave: a diffraction order.

34
00:01:57,463 --> 00:02:01,493
Colour sets the circle. Red light has a smaller circle than blue,

35
00:02:01,533 --> 00:02:05,085
so the same K bends red the most: a rainbow.

36
00:02:05,155 --> 00:02:07,423
Now shrink the period. K grows,

37
00:02:07,463 --> 00:02:10,495
and the orders leave the circle, one by one.

38
00:02:11,365 --> 00:02:14,554
At normal incidence into air, a period below the air

39
00:02:14,594 --> 00:02:17,564
wavelength leaves only the transmitted zero order.

40
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:19,800
Internal resonances can remain,

41
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:24,303
so treating the grating as a uniform film needs a separate check.

42
00:02:25,123 --> 00:02:28,520
Now let the structure repeat along the direction light travels:

43
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:31,457
a stack of layers, repeating every period.

44
00:02:31,527 --> 00:02:34,257
Shift it by one period and nothing changes.

45
00:02:34,297 --> 00:02:36,836
So the waves inside can only gain a

46
00:02:36,876 --> 00:02:39,357
fixed phase from one period to the next.

47
00:02:39,427 --> 00:02:45,200
These are Bloch waves: e^(iκz) times a function that repeats.

48
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:48,159
A full turn of phase per period is invisible,

49
00:02:48,199 --> 00:02:50,957
so κ only matters up to multiples of K.

50
00:02:51,027 --> 00:02:53,074
Plot frequency, ω, against κ.

51
00:02:53,114 --> 00:02:56,415
With no contrast, light follows a straight line,

52
00:02:56,455 --> 00:03:00,565
and its copies, shifted by K, are the same waves.

53
00:03:00,635 --> 00:03:04,220
The copies cross at the zone edge, where λ = 2nΛ.

54
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:09,683
That's the Bragg condition: reflections from every layer add up in step.

55
00:03:09,753 --> 00:03:13,763
Now turn up the index contrast, Δn. Watch the crossing:

56
00:03:13,803 --> 00:03:17,497
the forward and backward waves couple, and the bands pull apart.

57
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:21,031
A gap opens: a band of frequencies where

58
00:03:21,071 --> 00:03:23,081
no wave can travel through the stack.

59
00:03:23,151 --> 00:03:26,130
Light there dies off within a few layers, so it all reflects.

60
00:03:26,170 --> 00:03:30,789
Ten pairs make a nearly perfect mirror for one band of colours.

61
00:03:31,609 --> 00:03:34,613
How do you compute a real grating, with thick ridges?

62
00:03:34,653 --> 00:03:39,956
Rigorous coupled-wave analysis, RCWA, puts all of this together.

63
00:03:40,026 --> 00:03:42,899
Thanks to the period, the field in any layer is just

64
00:03:42,939 --> 00:03:45,990
a list of amplitudes, one per diffraction order.

65
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:50,456
In a uniform layer, each order travels alone, picking up its own phase,

66
00:03:50,496 --> 00:03:55,460
e^(ikz z). The matrix is diagonal, as in episode one.

67
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:59,092
In a grating layer, the Fourier coefficients of ε couple

68
00:03:59,132 --> 00:04:02,130
each order to the others, and the matrix fills in.

69
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:06,254
Diagonalize it: the eigenvectors are the layer's Bloch modes,

70
00:04:06,294 --> 00:04:08,496
and the eigenvalues give their kz.

71
00:04:08,566 --> 00:04:12,187
Advance each mode's phase, match the fields at every interface,

72
00:04:12,227 --> 00:04:14,604
and chain the layers with scattering matrices.

73
00:04:14,644 --> 00:04:18,919
They never contain growing exponentials, so they stay stable.

74
00:04:19,739 --> 00:04:24,182
On a computer, RCWA takes four lines: expand ε,

75
00:04:24,222 --> 00:04:27,690
build the matrix, diagonalize, chain the layers.

76
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:30,896
The pitfall is truncation: keep enough orders,

77
00:04:30,936 --> 00:04:33,354
and check that the answer converges.

78
00:04:33,394 --> 00:04:36,933
For the other polarization, use Li's factorization rules.

79
00:04:37,003 --> 00:04:40,355
The payoff: sweep the ridge width of a sub-wavelength grating,

80
00:04:40,395 --> 00:04:43,689
and record the phase of the light passing straight through.

81
00:04:44,159 --> 00:04:45,994
That's a metasurface's unit-cell library.

82
00:04:46,034 --> 00:04:48,465
Pick the width for each phase you need,

83
00:04:48,505 --> 00:04:51,440
and you have a flat lens, cell by cell.

84
00:04:52,260 --> 00:04:57,374
Symmetry fixes momentum. Flat interfaces conserve kx.

85
00:04:57,414 --> 00:05:02,181
Periodic ones conserve it only up to multiples of K = 2π/Λ,

86
00:05:02,221 --> 00:05:04,902
and where copies meet, gaps open.

87
00:05:04,972 --> 00:05:08,360
Where it breaks: RCWA needs true periodicity,

88
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:12,758
and a metasurface lens only borrows it locally, cell by cell.

89
00:05:12,828 --> 00:05:15,493
Total internal reflection traps light in a slab,

90
00:05:15,534 --> 00:05:17,532
and Bragg mirrors trap it between them.

91
00:05:17,572 --> 00:05:20,329
So how do we guide light, and store it?
